// `kryos test` and `kryos repl` both crash the whole process (Rust panic,
// rc=101, NOT a clean diagnostic) on the most basic possible struct
// literal. `kryos check`/`kryos run`/`kryos build --release` all accept
// this file and run it correctly -- this is specific to the two
// in-process-JIT-based commands.
//
// Repro:
//   kryos test tests/known_failures/test_repl_jit_missing_rt_symbols.kry
//
// Observed (HEAD, kryos 0.9.0):
//   running 1 @test function
//
//   thread 'kryos-main' (<pid>) panicked at
//   .../cranelift-jit-0.116.1/src/backend.rs:345:21:
//   can't resolve symbol kryos_calloc
//   note: run with `RUST_BACKTRACE=1` environment variable to display a backtrace
//   rc=101
//
// `kryos repl` crashes identically and interactively on the same two
// lines typed at the prompt:
//   struct Point { x: i64 y: i64 }
//   let p = Point { x: 1, y: 2 }
//
// Root cause (read, not just observed): `kryos test`/`kryos repl` both
// build an in-process `cranelift_jit::JITBuilder` via
// compiler/crates/kryos-codegen-cranelift/src/jit.rs, which registers a
// hand-maintained allowlist of ~120 `kryos_*` runtime symbols (string ops,
// map ops, ffi, a handful of named builtins). `kryos_calloc` -- the
// allocator EVERY struct/array construction lowers to -- is not in that
// list (grep confirms no `jit_builder.symbol("kryos_calloc", ...)` call
// exists anywhere in the file), so cranelift-jit's own linker aborts the
// process the moment it needs to resolve a call to it. `kryos run` does
// not hit this: per CLAUDE.md, `kryos run` is "AOT + subprocess, not an
// in-process JIT" -- it links a real object file against the full
// `kryos_rt.lib` static library (every exported symbol present), then
// executes a separate process. `kryos test`/`kryos repl` are the only two
// user-facing commands that go through jit.rs's partial symbol table.
//
// Severity: struct literals are one of the most basic constructs in the
// language (every non-trivial Kryos program uses them). This means
// `kryos test` cannot run ANY test that constructs a struct -- not a rare
// edge case, the common case -- and the failure mode is a raw Rust panic
// that kills the whole test binary (rc=101), not a per-test failure
// report; a file with N tests where only one constructs a struct loses
// all N results, not just the offending one. Also directly contradicts
// CLAUDE.md's own claim for `kryos test`: "Works with stdlib imports and
// @capabilities" -- confirmed false for `std::json`, `std::csv`, and
// `std::string::string_builder` (all crash with the same missing-symbol
// panic; `std::math`, which has no struct/array-allocating functions,
// does NOT crash, isolating the trigger to allocation, not imports in
// general). A second, narrower symptom of the same underlying gap was
// also observed: files importing `std::json` and calling `stringify`
// fail with a JIT VERIFIER error instead of a panic (`internal error:
// function \`__kry_pm_json__format_number\`: ... arg 0 has type f64,
// expected i64`) -- a distinct failure shape, not yet root-caused past
// "the same incomplete in-process JIT setup", filed here as the same
// class rather than a separate item.
//
// Ruled out: `kryos check` (rc=0), `kryos run` (rc=0, prints "3"),
// `kryos build --release` -- not re-verified this file specifically but
// consistent with `kryos run` sharing the AOT codegen path per
// docs/commands.md; only the in-process-JIT commands are affected.

struct Point {
    x: i64
    y: i64
}

@test
fn test_struct_alloc() {
    let p: Point = Point { x: 1, y: 2 }
    if p.x + p.y != 3 {
        throw "bad struct"
    }
}

fn main() {
    let p: Point = Point { x: 1, y: 2 }
    println(to_string(p.x + p.y))
}
